---
import BaseLayout from "@/components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy | AI Career Guide"
  description="How AI Career Guide researches, reviews, updates, and corrects its content."
>
  <article
    class="legal-page-premium max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 prose max-w-none prose-headings:text-[var(--color-text-primary)] prose-p:text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] prose-li:text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] prose-a:text-[var(--color-accent)]"
  >
    <p
      class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider !text-[var(--color-accent)]"
    >
      Editorial standards
    </p>
    <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
    <p>
      AI Career Guide aims to publish practical, accurate, useful, and durable
      material about AI, software engineering, projects, and technology careers.
      The goal is to help readers make better learning and engineering
      decisions, not simply publish content for search traffic.
    </p>
    <h2>Research and sources</h2>
    <p>
      Technical articles should use reliable primary documentation, standards,
      official project documentation, or other reputable sources when factual
      claims require verification. Source links are included on articles when
      available.
    </p>
    <h2>What we publish</h2>
    <p>
      The site prioritizes practical guides, learning roadmaps, engineering
      projects, technical references, and career preparation. Articles should
      explain the reasoning behind recommendations and should connect concepts
      to implementation, evaluation, or real engineering decisions where
      appropriate.
    </p>
    <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
    <p>
      When a claim can be checked directly, preference goes to primary sources:
      official documentation, standards, research papers, government or
      university material, and first-party engineering documentation.
      Established educational sites such as GeeksforGeeks, W3Schools, and
      roadmap.sh may be useful comparison and learning references, but they are
      not treated as substitutes for primary technical evidence.
    </p>
    <h2>Updates and reviews</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Published</strong> means the article was first released. <strong
        >Updated</strong
      > is reserved for meaningful content changes. <strong>Reviewed</strong> indicates
      an editorial check of the existing material; it does not imply that every external
      fact remains current indefinitely.
    </p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      When a material error is identified, the content should be corrected and,
      where appropriate, the update date changed. Broken links, outdated product
      details, incorrect code, and misleading technical claims are treated as
      legitimate corrections. Readers can use the <a href="/contact"
        >contact page</a
      > to report a specific issue with a source.
    </p>
    <h2>Affiliate and commercial content</h2>
    <p>
      Commercial relationships should never determine technical conclusions. Any
      affiliate or sponsored relationship will be disclosed where applicable.
    </p>
    <h2>Quality controls</h2>
    <p>
      Before publication, content should pass structural checks for metadata,
      links, images, sources, and build integrity. Technical examples should be
      reviewed for clarity and obvious correctness, and claims that materially
      influence a career or engineering decision should be presented with
      appropriate context and limitations.
    </p>
    <h2>AI-assisted writing</h2>
    <p>
      AI tools may assist with drafting, editing, research organization, or code
      examples, but published material is expected to be reviewed for accuracy
      and usefulness before release.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
